1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,349
A rally car can fly through the

2
00:00:02,349 --> 00:00:04,698
air for more than forty metres,

3
00:00:04,698 --> 00:00:07,199
land nose first on a gravel road,

4
00:00:07,199 --> 00:00:09,473
and keep going flat out to the

5
00:00:09,473 --> 00:00:12,202
next corner, as if nothing happened.

6
00:00:12,382 --> 00:00:14,831
Your road car would leave its wheels

7
00:00:14,831 --> 00:00:15,307
behind.

8
00:00:15,487 --> 00:00:18,181
The difference is not luck or magic.

9
00:00:18,361 --> 00:00:20,283
It is a list of parts built

10
00:00:20,283 --> 00:00:22,633
for the worst roads in the world.

11
00:00:22,813 --> 00:00:26,024
Today, ten parts of a rally car,

12
00:00:26,024 --> 00:00:28,433
and why each one exists.

13
00:00:28,782 --> 00:00:31,836
Rally is motorsport on real roads.

14
00:00:32,016 --> 00:00:33,829
Gravel in Finland,

15
00:00:33,829 --> 00:00:35,341
snow in Sweden,

16
00:00:35,341 --> 00:00:36,650
mud in Kenya,

17
00:00:36,650 --> 00:00:38,565
tarmac in the Alps,

18
00:00:38,565 --> 00:00:41,083
often in the same season.

19
00:00:41,263 --> 00:00:43,425
The roads are closed for the stage,

20
00:00:43,425 --> 00:00:45,402
but they are not prepared like a

21
00:00:45,402 --> 00:00:46,081
race track.

22
00:00:46,261 --> 00:00:49,358
Rocks, ruts, crests and jumps are part

23
00:00:49,358 --> 00:00:50,499
of the course.

24
00:00:50,679 --> 00:00:52,715
And the cars race one at a

25
00:00:52,715 --> 00:00:54,594
time, against the clock,

26
00:00:54,594 --> 00:00:57,726
with a co-driver reading the road ahead.

27
00:00:58,077 --> 00:01:01,122
As always, every fact here comes from

28
00:01:01,122 --> 00:01:02,356
a named source,

29
00:01:02,356 --> 00:01:05,731
the FIA, the World Rally Championship and

30
00:01:05,731 --> 00:01:10,093
motorsport references, all listed in the description.

31
00:01:10,273 --> 00:01:12,270
Let's look under the car first.

32
00:01:12,620 --> 00:01:14,718
Why don't rally cars break when they

33
00:01:14,718 --> 00:01:15,418
land a jump?

34
00:01:15,598 --> 00:01:17,409
Because of the suspension.

35
00:01:17,589 --> 00:01:20,194
A gravel rally car has far more

36
00:01:20,194 --> 00:01:23,052
suspension travel than a road car,

37
00:01:23,052 --> 00:01:25,994
often more than twenty centimetres.

38
00:01:26,174 --> 00:01:27,209
When it lands,

39
00:01:27,209 --> 00:01:29,352
the wheels can move up a long

40
00:01:29,352 --> 00:01:32,235
way into the body before anything hits.

41
00:01:32,415 --> 00:01:35,433
The springs and dampers absorb the energy

42
00:01:35,433 --> 00:01:37,642
of a landing that would bend a

43
00:01:37,642 --> 00:01:38,452
normal car.

44
00:01:38,801 --> 00:01:40,647
The dampers are the secret.

45
00:01:40,827 --> 00:01:41,814
They are big,

46
00:01:41,814 --> 00:01:45,760
adjustable units, often costing thousands of dollars

47
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:49,402
each, with separate settings for slow movements,

48
00:01:49,402 --> 00:01:51,982
like the body rolling in a corner,

49
00:01:51,982 --> 00:01:53,424
and fast movements,

50
00:01:53,424 --> 00:01:55,549
like a wheel hitting a rock.

51
00:01:55,729 --> 00:01:58,819
Teams change the settings for every rally,

52
00:01:58,819 --> 00:02:00,953
and sometimes during the day.

53
00:02:01,133 --> 00:02:04,094
A rally car on gravel rides high,

54
00:02:04,094 --> 00:02:05,977
soft and long legged,

55
00:02:05,977 --> 00:02:08,668
almost like an off road truck.

56
00:02:09,019 --> 00:02:11,122
The same car on tarmac is a

57
00:02:11,122 --> 00:02:12,525
different machine.

58
00:02:12,705 --> 00:02:14,466
For asphalt rallies,

59
00:02:14,466 --> 00:02:16,402
the teams fit shorter,

60
00:02:16,402 --> 00:02:20,012
stiffer suspension, lower the car and fit

61
00:02:20,012 --> 00:02:21,157
wider wheels.

62
00:02:21,337 --> 00:02:24,535
Engineers can turn a gravel car into

63
00:02:24,535 --> 00:02:27,467
a tarmac car in the service park.

64
00:02:27,647 --> 00:02:29,715
The jumps in Finland are where the

65
00:02:29,715 --> 00:02:31,478
gravel setup earns its money.

66
00:02:31,658 --> 00:02:34,364
Some crests send the cars flying for

67
00:02:34,364 --> 00:02:36,093
more than forty metres.

68
00:02:36,443 --> 00:02:38,763
Why is the inside of a rally

69
00:02:38,763 --> 00:02:40,751
car full of metal pipes?

70
00:02:40,931 --> 00:02:42,490
This is the roll cage,

71
00:02:42,490 --> 00:02:44,686
steel tubes welded to the body,

72
00:02:44,686 --> 00:02:46,458
running around the doors,

73
00:02:46,458 --> 00:02:48,583
over the heads of the crew and

74
00:02:48,583 --> 00:02:49,859
down to the floor.

75
00:02:50,039 --> 00:02:52,311
When a rally car rolls over,

76
00:02:52,311 --> 00:02:53,285
and many do,

77
00:02:53,285 --> 00:02:56,451
the cage keeps the roof from collapsing

78
00:02:56,451 --> 00:02:58,886
onto the driver and co-driver.

79
00:02:59,066 --> 00:03:01,318
It also makes the whole body much

80
00:03:01,318 --> 00:03:01,864
stiffer.

81
00:03:02,214 --> 00:03:05,122
The cage is built to strict FIA

82
00:03:05,122 --> 00:03:05,685
rules.

83
00:03:05,865 --> 00:03:09,547
The tubes have minimum sizes and thicknesses,

84
00:03:09,547 --> 00:03:12,657
and the design is tested and approved.

85
00:03:12,837 --> 00:03:14,162
In modern cars,

86
00:03:14,162 --> 00:03:17,520
there are extra bars inside the doors,

87
00:03:17,520 --> 00:03:21,230
because side impacts against trees are one

88
00:03:21,230 --> 00:03:24,853
of the most dangerous accidents in rally.

89
00:03:25,033 --> 00:03:28,282
A road car is designed to crumple

90
00:03:28,282 --> 00:03:30,153
and absorb a crash.

91
00:03:30,333 --> 00:03:33,407
A rally car is designed to survive

92
00:03:33,407 --> 00:03:36,301
many crashes in a single season.

93
00:03:36,651 --> 00:03:40,459
Safety in rally changed forever in nineteen

94
00:03:40,459 --> 00:03:41,434
eighty-six.

95
00:03:41,614 --> 00:03:45,169
That year, Henri Toivonen and his co-driver

96
00:03:45,169 --> 00:03:48,641
Sergio Cresto died when their Lancia Delta

97
00:03:48,641 --> 00:03:51,204
S four left the road in Corsica

98
00:03:51,204 --> 00:03:52,527
and caught fire.

99
00:03:52,707 --> 00:03:55,592
The extreme Group B cars were banned

100
00:03:55,592 --> 00:03:57,676
at the end of that season.

101
00:03:57,856 --> 00:03:59,535
Since then, cages,

102
00:03:59,535 --> 00:04:03,360
fuel tanks and seats have become stronger

103
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:05,972
with every new set of rules.

104
00:04:06,322 --> 00:04:08,426
Why do rally cars have a metal

105
00:04:08,426 --> 00:04:09,619
plate underneath?

106
00:04:09,799 --> 00:04:11,693
It is the sump guard,

107
00:04:11,693 --> 00:04:14,129
also called the skid plate.

108
00:04:14,309 --> 00:04:17,709
A thick plate of aluminium or composite

109
00:04:17,709 --> 00:04:19,279
covers the engine,

110
00:04:19,279 --> 00:04:22,853
the gearbox and the important pipes under

111
00:04:22,853 --> 00:04:23,551
the car.

112
00:04:23,731 --> 00:04:25,247
On a gravel road,

113
00:04:25,247 --> 00:04:27,299
a rally car hits rocks,

114
00:04:27,299 --> 00:04:30,778
ruts and the ground itself after jumps.

115
00:04:30,958 --> 00:04:32,187
Without the guard,

116
00:04:32,187 --> 00:04:34,236
one rock could punch a hole in

117
00:04:34,236 --> 00:04:34,987
the engine.

118
00:04:35,337 --> 00:04:38,088
The plate is designed to slide.

119
00:04:38,268 --> 00:04:40,848
When the car lands hard or scrapes

120
00:04:40,848 --> 00:04:41,835
over a crest,

121
00:04:41,835 --> 00:04:44,264
the guard takes the hit and lets

122
00:04:44,264 --> 00:04:47,223
the car glide over the obstacle instead

123
00:04:47,223 --> 00:04:48,589
of catching on it.

124
00:04:48,769 --> 00:04:50,196
After each stage,

125
00:04:50,196 --> 00:04:53,722
mechanics often find deep scratches in the

126
00:04:53,722 --> 00:04:57,667
plate, and sometimes they replace it completely

127
00:04:57,667 --> 00:04:59,346
in the service park.

128
00:04:59,526 --> 00:05:02,249
It is the part that sacrifices itself

129
00:05:02,249 --> 00:05:04,530
so the engine does not have to.

130
00:05:04,880 --> 00:05:06,969
Even the fuel tank is protected.

131
00:05:07,149 --> 00:05:08,947
In a modern rally car,

132
00:05:08,947 --> 00:05:11,480
the fuel sits in a safety cell,

133
00:05:11,480 --> 00:05:14,341
a flexible bag inside a strong box,

134
00:05:14,341 --> 00:05:17,119
placed where it is least likely to

135
00:05:17,119 --> 00:05:17,691
be hit.

136
00:05:17,871 --> 00:05:20,562
Fire was the great danger in the

137
00:05:20,562 --> 00:05:20,983
past.

138
00:05:21,163 --> 00:05:23,586
Today the most important parts of the

139
00:05:23,586 --> 00:05:26,140
car are hidden behind layers of armour,

140
00:05:26,140 --> 00:05:27,908
from the floor to the roof.

141
00:05:28,258 --> 00:05:31,014
Why do rally cars have big flaps

142
00:05:31,014 --> 00:05:32,565
behind the wheels?

143
00:05:32,745 --> 00:05:36,329
Because spinning tyres on gravel throw stones

144
00:05:36,329 --> 00:05:37,842
like a machine gun.

145
00:05:38,022 --> 00:05:41,005
A rally car accelerating on a loose

146
00:05:41,005 --> 00:05:44,755
surface fires rocks backwards at high speed.

147
00:05:44,935 --> 00:05:46,793
The mud flaps catch them.

148
00:05:46,973 --> 00:05:49,430
The rules require them on gravel,

149
00:05:49,430 --> 00:05:51,665
to protect the car's own body,

150
00:05:51,665 --> 00:05:54,345
and everyone standing near the road.

151
00:05:54,696 --> 00:05:57,717
Rally fans often stand very close to

152
00:05:57,717 --> 00:06:00,402
the stages, and the cars pass at

153
00:06:00,402 --> 00:06:01,325
full speed.

154
00:06:01,505 --> 00:06:03,967
A stone thrown from a wheel can

155
00:06:03,967 --> 00:06:04,999
be dangerous.

156
00:06:05,179 --> 00:06:06,638
The flaps are thick,

157
00:06:06,638 --> 00:06:09,336
flexible and long enough to stop most

158
00:06:09,336 --> 00:06:10,358
of the gravel.

159
00:06:10,538 --> 00:06:13,307
They also stop the car from blasting

160
00:06:13,307 --> 00:06:16,230
its own rear bodywork and windows with

161
00:06:16,230 --> 00:06:19,153
stones, which would break parts of the

162
00:06:19,153 --> 00:06:20,615
car during a stage.

163
00:06:20,965 --> 00:06:23,507
The flaps are also a canvas.

164
00:06:23,687 --> 00:06:26,414
Teams put sponsor names and the car's

165
00:06:26,414 --> 00:06:27,519
number on them,

166
00:06:27,519 --> 00:06:30,467
because they are big flat surfaces right

167
00:06:30,467 --> 00:06:32,604
at the height of the cameras.

168
00:06:32,784 --> 00:06:33,955
On a muddy rally,

169
00:06:33,955 --> 00:06:36,090
they are often the only part of

170
00:06:36,090 --> 00:06:37,950
the car you can still read.

171
00:06:38,130 --> 00:06:41,135
A strip of rubber with two jobs,

172
00:06:41,135 --> 00:06:44,516
safety first and advertising second.

173
00:06:44,866 --> 00:06:46,886
Why do some rally cars have a

174
00:06:46,886 --> 00:06:49,254
giant set of lights on the bonnet?

175
00:06:49,434 --> 00:06:52,571
Because some stages are run at night,

176
00:06:52,571 --> 00:06:55,963
on narrow roads in forests or mountains,

177
00:06:55,963 --> 00:06:57,828
with no street lights.

178
00:06:58,008 --> 00:07:00,805
A normal car's headlights are not enough

179
00:07:00,805 --> 00:07:03,671
at more than one hundred fifty kilometres

180
00:07:03,671 --> 00:07:04,301
per hour.

181
00:07:04,481 --> 00:07:07,858
The light pod carries several powerful lamps,

182
00:07:07,858 --> 00:07:09,810
aimed at different angles,

183
00:07:09,810 --> 00:07:12,136
to light the road far ahead and

184
00:07:12,136 --> 00:07:14,013
the edges of the corners.

185
00:07:14,363 --> 00:07:16,092
Each lamp has a job.

186
00:07:16,272 --> 00:07:17,840
Some throw a long,

187
00:07:17,840 --> 00:07:20,365
narrow beam to see far down a

188
00:07:20,365 --> 00:07:21,149
straight.

189
00:07:21,329 --> 00:07:22,563
Others are wide,

190
00:07:22,563 --> 00:07:25,108
to light the inside of the corner

191
00:07:25,108 --> 00:07:26,959
the car is turning into.

192
00:07:27,139 --> 00:07:29,581
The pod is bolted onto the front

193
00:07:29,581 --> 00:07:32,328
for night stages and removed for day

194
00:07:32,328 --> 00:07:35,533
stages, to save weight and air resistance.

195
00:07:35,713 --> 00:07:37,375
Modern pods use LEDs,

196
00:07:37,375 --> 00:07:40,460
which are lighter and brighter than the

197
00:07:40,460 --> 00:07:41,251
old lamps.

198
00:07:41,602 --> 00:07:43,930
Night stages are some of the most

199
00:07:43,930 --> 00:07:45,201
dramatic in rally.

200
00:07:45,381 --> 00:07:47,232
At the Monte Carlo rally,

201
00:07:47,232 --> 00:07:49,453
crews may face snow and ice in

202
00:07:49,453 --> 00:07:51,453
the mountains, in the dark.

203
00:07:51,633 --> 00:07:54,595
Fans line the roads with torches and

204
00:07:54,595 --> 00:07:55,418
campfires.

205
00:07:55,598 --> 00:07:57,971
The drivers say the hardest part is

206
00:07:57,971 --> 00:07:59,464
not seeing far enough.

207
00:07:59,644 --> 00:08:01,594
The light pod is their only way

208
00:08:01,594 --> 00:08:03,733
to see the next corner before they

209
00:08:03,733 --> 00:08:04,299
reach it.

210
00:08:04,650 --> 00:08:06,788
Why is there an air scoop on

211
00:08:06,788 --> 00:08:08,620
the roof of a rally car?

212
00:08:08,800 --> 00:08:10,565
To keep the crew from cooking.

213
00:08:10,745 --> 00:08:13,576
The windows of a rally car stay

214
00:08:13,576 --> 00:08:15,494
closed on the stages,

215
00:08:15,494 --> 00:08:18,508
because open windows let in dust,

216
00:08:18,508 --> 00:08:20,335
stones and branches.

217
00:08:20,515 --> 00:08:23,765
The engine, the gearbox and the exhaust

218
00:08:23,765 --> 00:08:26,015
are all close to the cabin,

219
00:08:26,015 --> 00:08:28,849
and on a hot rally the temperature

220
00:08:28,849 --> 00:08:31,266
inside can be extremely high.

221
00:08:31,446 --> 00:08:34,250
The scoop forces fresh air into the

222
00:08:34,250 --> 00:08:34,731
cabin.

223
00:08:35,082 --> 00:08:37,140
The roof is the best place for

224
00:08:37,140 --> 00:08:37,345
it.

225
00:08:37,525 --> 00:08:40,091
The air there is clean and fast,

226
00:08:40,091 --> 00:08:42,576
away from the dust thrown up by

227
00:08:42,576 --> 00:08:43,458
the wheels.

228
00:08:43,638 --> 00:08:46,195
The scoop pushes the air down through

229
00:08:46,195 --> 00:08:47,854
a vent into the cockpit,

230
00:08:47,854 --> 00:08:50,203
where it blows over the driver and

231
00:08:50,203 --> 00:08:50,894
co-driver.

232
00:08:51,074 --> 00:08:53,138
In rallies like Mexico,

233
00:08:53,138 --> 00:08:54,574
Kenya or Greece,

234
00:08:54,574 --> 00:08:56,906
where the heat is intense,

235
00:08:56,906 --> 00:08:59,777
that flow of air helps them stay

236
00:08:59,777 --> 00:09:02,290
focused through long stages.

237
00:09:02,640 --> 00:09:05,846
A tired, overheated driver makes mistakes,

238
00:09:05,846 --> 00:09:08,059
and on a rally road a mistake

239
00:09:08,059 --> 00:09:09,280
can mean a tree.

240
00:09:09,460 --> 00:09:11,176
So the cooling of the crew is

241
00:09:11,176 --> 00:09:13,660
treated almost as seriously as the cooling

242
00:09:13,660 --> 00:09:14,488
of the engine.

243
00:09:14,668 --> 00:09:16,898
A small scoop on the roof is

244
00:09:16,898 --> 00:09:19,846
there to cool the most important part

245
00:09:19,846 --> 00:09:20,722
of the car,

246
00:09:20,722 --> 00:09:22,156
the people inside.

247
00:09:22,506 --> 00:09:24,447
Why do rally cars have a tall

248
00:09:24,447 --> 00:09:26,790
handbrake lever next to the driver?

249
00:09:26,970 --> 00:09:29,453
It is a hydraulic handbrake,

250
00:09:29,453 --> 00:09:31,316
used to turn the car,

251
00:09:31,316 --> 00:09:32,647
not to park it.

252
00:09:32,827 --> 00:09:34,186
In a tight hairpin,

253
00:09:34,186 --> 00:09:36,117
the driver pulls the lever,

254
00:09:36,117 --> 00:09:38,691
the rear wheels lock for an instant,

255
00:09:38,691 --> 00:09:40,836
and the back of the car swings

256
00:09:40,836 --> 00:09:41,337
around.

257
00:09:41,517 --> 00:09:43,934
The car rotates much faster than it

258
00:09:43,934 --> 00:09:45,592
would by steering alone.

259
00:09:45,942 --> 00:09:48,030
The lever is tall so the driver

260
00:09:48,030 --> 00:09:49,378
can grab it quickly,

261
00:09:49,378 --> 00:09:52,275
without looking, while the other hand stays

262
00:09:52,275 --> 00:09:53,151
on the wheel.

263
00:09:53,331 --> 00:09:55,504
In a four wheel drive rally car,

264
00:09:55,504 --> 00:09:58,220
pulling it also disconnects the drive to

265
00:09:58,220 --> 00:10:00,189
the rear wheels for a moment,

266
00:10:00,189 --> 00:10:02,701
so they can lock without fighting the

267
00:10:02,701 --> 00:10:03,177
engine.

268
00:10:03,357 --> 00:10:05,861
Let go, and the drive comes back

269
00:10:05,861 --> 00:10:08,210
as the car points to the exit.

270
00:10:08,560 --> 00:10:10,440
Drivers use it differently.

271
00:10:10,620 --> 00:10:12,793
Some use it in many corners,

272
00:10:12,793 --> 00:10:15,508
some only in the tightest hairpins.

273
00:10:15,688 --> 00:10:17,818
On the narrowest mountain roads,

274
00:10:17,818 --> 00:10:19,482
it can be the only way to

275
00:10:19,482 --> 00:10:22,410
turn the car without stopping and reversing.

276
00:10:22,590 --> 00:10:24,916
A parking brake on your car holds

277
00:10:24,916 --> 00:10:25,621
you still.

278
00:10:25,801 --> 00:10:27,143
On a rally car,

279
00:10:27,143 --> 00:10:29,738
the same idea is used to make

280
00:10:29,738 --> 00:10:30,990
the car dance.

281
00:10:31,341 --> 00:10:33,249
Why do rally cars bang,

282
00:10:33,249 --> 00:10:36,402
pop and shoot flames from the exhaust?

283
00:10:36,582 --> 00:10:38,614
It is the anti-lag system.

284
00:10:38,794 --> 00:10:41,174
A turbo engine has a delay.

285
00:10:41,354 --> 00:10:44,586
When the driver lifts off the throttle,

286
00:10:44,586 --> 00:10:46,327
the turbo slows down,

287
00:10:46,327 --> 00:10:48,813
and when he accelerates again,

288
00:10:48,813 --> 00:10:51,299
it takes a moment to spin back

289
00:10:51,299 --> 00:10:51,548
up.

290
00:10:51,728 --> 00:10:53,539
On a twisty rally road,

291
00:10:53,539 --> 00:10:56,611
with constant braking and accelerating,

292
00:10:56,611 --> 00:10:58,973
that delay would cost a lot of

293
00:10:58,973 --> 00:10:59,367
time.

294
00:10:59,718 --> 00:11:02,801
Anti-lag keeps the turbo spinning even when

295
00:11:02,801 --> 00:11:05,023
the driver is off the throttle.

296
00:11:05,203 --> 00:11:07,805
The engine lets a little air through

297
00:11:07,805 --> 00:11:09,178
and adds fuel late,

298
00:11:09,178 --> 00:11:11,563
so it burns in the exhaust before

299
00:11:11,563 --> 00:11:12,285
the turbo.

300
00:11:12,465 --> 00:11:15,443
The explosions keep the turbo turning at

301
00:11:15,443 --> 00:11:16,262
high speed.

302
00:11:16,442 --> 00:11:19,552
When the driver touches the throttle again,

303
00:11:19,552 --> 00:11:21,794
the boost is there immediately.

304
00:11:21,974 --> 00:11:24,231
The bangs you hear in the forest

305
00:11:24,231 --> 00:11:25,712
are those explosions.

306
00:11:26,063 --> 00:11:27,990
It is brutal for the hardware.

307
00:11:28,170 --> 00:11:31,451
The exhaust and the turbo get extremely

308
00:11:31,451 --> 00:11:34,480
hot, and parts wear much faster than

309
00:11:34,480 --> 00:11:35,658
in a road car.

310
00:11:35,838 --> 00:11:38,630
That is why anti-lag is turned off

311
00:11:38,630 --> 00:11:41,586
on the road sections between stages,

312
00:11:41,586 --> 00:11:44,952
where rally cars drive in normal traffic.

313
00:11:45,132 --> 00:11:47,221
It is a system built for a

314
00:11:47,221 --> 00:11:49,470
few minutes of total attack,

315
00:11:49,470 --> 00:11:51,076
not for a long life.

316
00:11:51,427 --> 00:11:54,434
Why do rally tyres have metal spikes?

317
00:11:54,614 --> 00:11:57,637
These are the studded tyres used on

318
00:11:57,637 --> 00:11:58,760
snow and ice,

319
00:11:58,760 --> 00:12:01,092
especially at Rally Sweden.

320
00:12:01,272 --> 00:12:04,259
Hundreds of small metal studs stick out

321
00:12:04,259 --> 00:12:05,254
of each tyre.

322
00:12:05,434 --> 00:12:08,378
On ice, a normal tyre has almost

323
00:12:08,378 --> 00:12:09,114
no grip.

324
00:12:09,294 --> 00:12:11,602
The studs dig into the ice like

325
00:12:11,602 --> 00:12:13,314
the claws of an animal,

326
00:12:13,314 --> 00:12:15,919
giving the car grip in places where

327
00:12:15,919 --> 00:12:17,630
you could barely stand.

328
00:12:17,981 --> 00:12:21,134
With studs, rally cars race on frozen

329
00:12:21,134 --> 00:12:24,542
roads at speeds above one hundred eighty

330
00:12:24,542 --> 00:12:26,247
kilometres per hour.

331
00:12:26,427 --> 00:12:29,383
In Sweden, the roads are lined with

332
00:12:29,383 --> 00:12:32,424
snow banks, and drivers lean the car

333
00:12:32,424 --> 00:12:34,451
against them in corners,

334
00:12:34,451 --> 00:12:36,562
using the snow as a wall.

335
00:12:36,742 --> 00:12:39,524
The studs wear down during a stage,

336
00:12:39,524 --> 00:12:42,147
and the tyre rules limit how many

337
00:12:42,147 --> 00:12:44,690
studs each tyre can have and how

338
00:12:44,690 --> 00:12:46,518
far they may stick out.

339
00:12:46,868 --> 00:12:50,154
Tyres are the only contact between the

340
00:12:50,154 --> 00:12:51,624
car and the road,

341
00:12:51,624 --> 00:12:55,084
so rally teams carry different tyres for

342
00:12:55,084 --> 00:12:56,295
every surface.

343
00:12:56,475 --> 00:12:59,608
Soft and hard compounds for gravel,

344
00:12:59,608 --> 00:13:02,832
slicks and grooved tyres for tarmac,

345
00:13:02,832 --> 00:13:04,801
narrow tyres for snow,

346
00:13:04,801 --> 00:13:06,413
and studs for ice.

347
00:13:06,593 --> 00:13:07,780
On a mixed rally,

348
00:13:07,780 --> 00:13:10,432
choosing the wrong tyre in the morning

349
00:13:10,432 --> 00:13:12,177
can decide the whole day.

350
00:13:12,528 --> 00:13:15,026
Why is the passenger in a rally

351
00:13:15,026 --> 00:13:18,007
car reading a notebook at full speed?

352
00:13:18,187 --> 00:13:20,137
These are the pace notes,

353
00:13:20,137 --> 00:13:22,866
and the passenger is the co-driver.

354
00:13:23,046 --> 00:13:24,354
Before the rally,

355
00:13:24,354 --> 00:13:26,969
the crew drives each stage slowly,

356
00:13:26,969 --> 00:13:29,892
writing a description of every corner,

357
00:13:29,892 --> 00:13:31,046
crest and jump.

358
00:13:31,226 --> 00:13:32,560
During the stage,

359
00:13:32,560 --> 00:13:35,623
the co-driver reads the notes out loud,

360
00:13:35,623 --> 00:13:37,193
a few seconds ahead,

361
00:13:37,193 --> 00:13:39,863
so the driver knows what is coming

362
00:13:39,863 --> 00:13:41,512
before he can see it.

363
00:13:41,862 --> 00:13:43,696
The notes are written in a code.

364
00:13:43,876 --> 00:13:46,880
Many crews use numbers for how tight

365
00:13:46,880 --> 00:13:48,132
each corner is,

366
00:13:48,132 --> 00:13:50,468
along with words like crest,

367
00:13:50,468 --> 00:13:52,471
don't cut, or over jump,

368
00:13:52,471 --> 00:13:55,892
and the distance to the next instruction.

369
00:13:56,072 --> 00:13:58,336
A sentence like six left,

370
00:13:58,336 --> 00:13:59,784
into four right,

371
00:13:59,784 --> 00:14:03,496
don't cut, describes a corner sequence in

372
00:14:03,496 --> 00:14:04,582
two seconds.

373
00:14:04,762 --> 00:14:07,790
The driver trusts the voice so completely

374
00:14:07,790 --> 00:14:10,375
that he drives flat out over crests

375
00:14:10,375 --> 00:14:11,926
he cannot see beyond.

376
00:14:12,276 --> 00:14:14,123
A mistake in the notes or in

377
00:14:14,123 --> 00:14:16,300
the timing of the reading can put

378
00:14:16,300 --> 00:14:17,686
the car off the road.

379
00:14:17,866 --> 00:14:20,345
That is why the co-driver is as

380
00:14:20,345 --> 00:14:22,264
important as the driver,

381
00:14:22,264 --> 00:14:25,223
and why crews often stay together for

382
00:14:25,223 --> 00:14:25,703
years.

383
00:14:25,883 --> 00:14:28,354
The fastest part of a rally car

384
00:14:28,354 --> 00:14:31,223
is not the engine or the suspension.

385
00:14:31,403 --> 00:14:34,375
It is the conversation inside the cockpit.

386
00:14:34,726 --> 00:14:36,386
So here are the ten.

387
00:14:36,566 --> 00:14:39,399
Long suspension that swallows jumps.

388
00:14:39,579 --> 00:14:41,892
A roll cage for when the car

389
00:14:41,892 --> 00:14:42,388
rolls.

390
00:14:42,568 --> 00:14:44,122
Armour under the car,

391
00:14:44,122 --> 00:14:45,676
flaps for the stones,

392
00:14:45,676 --> 00:14:48,044
lights for the night and a scoop

393
00:14:48,044 --> 00:14:49,894
on the roof for the crew.

394
00:14:50,074 --> 00:14:51,656
A handbrake to turn,

395
00:14:51,656 --> 00:14:54,029
an anti-lag system that bangs,

396
00:14:54,029 --> 00:14:55,532
spikes for the ice,

397
00:14:55,532 --> 00:14:58,300
and a notebook that lets the driver

398
00:14:58,300 --> 00:14:59,803
see around corners.

399
00:15:00,154 --> 00:15:02,695
A rally car looks like a normal

400
00:15:02,695 --> 00:15:04,171
car with stickers.

401
00:15:04,351 --> 00:15:07,888
Underneath, almost every part has been rebuilt

402
00:15:07,888 --> 00:15:10,887
to survive the roughest roads on Earth.

403
00:15:11,067 --> 00:15:14,109
If you want more machines explained part

404
00:15:14,109 --> 00:15:17,000
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

405
00:15:17,180 --> 00:15:19,166
And tell us in the comments which

406
00:15:19,166 --> 00:15:21,151
machine you want us to take apart

407
00:15:21,151 --> 00:15:21,452
next.
